    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Project KO11 Is Proof That Geely Wants To \"Knock Out\" Range Rover, Launches Next Year in Europe\nNew Ferrari Luce EV Coming Stateside From $560,800\n9 photos\nPhoto: Ferrari / edited\nMade in Maranello, the first-ever Ferrari with electric-only propulsion is naturally coming stateside. According to Car and Driver, prospective customers in the US will have to spend $560,800 at the very least for the five-seater Fezza.\nEnormous, isn't it? Thing is, Ferrari could have been greedier. Nearly $120,000 pricier than the Purosangue, the Luce also has rear doors and proper back seats. Any other similarity doesn't really matter, for the Luce targets very different customers who lean more into techy stuff than V12s and motorsports.\nWhen the Luce broke cover, the cited publication expected a starting price in the ballpark of $640,000 when converting from euros. The official sticker is nearly $80,000 under that projection, yet Ferrari's newcomer still clears the Rolls-Royce Cullinan. While it may have been developed with BMW money, ultra-luxury SUVs don't get better than said British colossus from Goodwood.\nTouted as the priciest five-seat vehicle for 2027, the Ferrari Luce is more attention magnet for all the wrong reasons than Prancing Horse that will be fondly remembered in a decade's time. Certain peeps believe that what hides underneath the sheetmetal is a technological flex, but Chinese automakers like Denza can do much better for less than $175,000 when converting from yuan.\nInstead of the Purosangue's twelve cylinders, four electric motors are on the menu here. That's one per wheel, and their combined output is 1,035 mechanical horsepower. If you know your high-performance BEVs well, that means the Luce is only 16 ponies up on the lower-slung Porsche Taycan Turbo GT. At $243,700 before options, the Porker is just around twice as affordable.\nPhoto: Ferrari Mounting a motor at each corner lets the Luce meter torque independently, which is especially interesting when it comes to cornering. Equipped with 23- and 24-inch wheels, the zero-emission model further sweetens the go-faster deal with active front aero that enables a lower drag coefficient than any other Ferrari road car.\nUnderpinned by a dedicated EV platform, the Luce runs about two inches longer overall than the Purosangue. It also sits two inches lower. While its center of gravity is better, the high-voltage battery and its wheel-mounted electric drive units add up. The kerb weight of 2,260 kilograms (4,982 pounds) makes the Luce the heaviest Ferrari ever, and regardless of driving aids, mass will always take away some of the driving experience's quality.\nBecause Maranello created the Ferrari of electric vehicles with the Luce, it's hardly surprising that driving range is on the meh side. The Prancing Horse claims up to 530 kilometers (329 miles) in the WLTP from a 122-kWh structural pack that gladly takes 800 volts. Hard numbers aside, Maranello wants the Luce experienced as an electric Ferrari rather than a Ferrari doing an impression of classic models we all know and love.\nCarrying one of the loudest design-focused debates in recent automotive history, the Luce wouldn't have been possible without shared input from Jony Ive and Marc Newson. Its design language isn't likely to influence Ferrari Centro Stile for the next generation of combustion and electrified models, which require a different stylistic direction to differentiate themselves from the all-electric lineup.\nA car that many among us like to hate, including yours truly, the Luce is a commercial success for the time being. The entire 2026 allocation was fully spoken for in under two months after the May reveal in Rome, and the order books already cover 2027 in full. Longer term, Luce targets 2,500 deliveries by 2030, meaning 600-something units on average per annum.\nVia // Car and Driver\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nFerrari Luce 2027 Ferrari Luce Ferrari EV 2028 ferrari luce price\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Tesla Stock Falters Despite Better-Than-Expected Q3 Earnings, It's Still a Carmaker",
